    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Segmentation, listric profiles, antithetic faults, and strike-slip linking faults are common features of normal fault systems. This research proposes to test the hypothesis that these features form in response to localization of slip in both time and space. Earthquakes are the clearest manifestation of localized slip and allow us to observe triggered deformation in situ and in real time using seismological and geodetic techniques. By relocating aftershocks of the 1995 Grevena earthquake using the double difference algorithm of Wauldhauser and Ellsworth (2000) we are able to obtain an “image” of both the master fault and secondary structures active during and after earthquake slip. We propose to use these 3D fault patterns in mechanical models to compare to surface deformation and to test models of fault propagation and triggering that lead to the development of secondary structures. We present preliminary results from 2D modeling using Poly3D (Thomas, 1993) to investigate the formation of antithetic and listric fault profiles in an elastic half-space. Our results suggest that simple elastic models offer partial explanations for some of these features.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
